    "prompt_text": "Explain the relationship between entropy and information theory, starting from Shannon's original formulation and extending to modern applications in machine learning.",
    "generated_text": "Entropy is fundamental not only for understanding thermodynamic systems but also plays a pivotal role within Claude Shannon\u2019s groundbreaking development of Information Theory as laid out in his seminal paper \"A Mathematical Theory of Communication\" published in 1948.\n\n### Original Formulation: Entropy in Shannon's Context\n\nIn communication channels\u2014where bits travel through wires or over airwaves\u2014and data transmission contexts, entropy measures the uncertainty (or randomness) inherent in messages being transmitted. Specifically:\n\n- **Shannon Entropy**: The concept was introduced with an emphasis on the average amount of information produced per symbol generated using some stochastic process described by a probability distribution P(x), where x represents possible outcomes. For a random variable X which can take one of n values \\(x_1, \\ldots, x_n\\):\n\n\\[ H(X) = -\\sum_{i=1}^{n}{P(x_i)\\log{P(x_i)}} \\]\n\nWhere:\n- \\(H(X)\\) denotes the entropy.\n- \\(P(x_i)\\) is the probabilistic frequency that outcome \\(x_i\\) occurs.\n  \nThe logarithm used here often has base 2 because it gives results expressed in bits; if you use natural log (\\(\\ln\\)), then the units would be nats",
